<main data-pagefind-body="" id="main"><section class="hero" id="home"><div class="hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">A LIVING HERITAGE. AN OPEN INVITATION.</p><h1>Ancient wisdom.<br/>Endless <em>discovery.</em></h1><p class="hero-description">Enter the world of Sanatan <a class="glossary-link" href="/glossary.html#dharma" title="dharma: glossary definition">Dharma</a> through its ideas, traditions and the stories that carry them across generations.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="/understanding.html">Begin exploring</a><a class="text-link" href="/stories.html">Find your next story</a></div><div class="hero-note"><span lang="hi">सनातन</span><p>Stories, wisdom and traditions<br/>of Sanatan Dharma</p></div></div><figure class="hero-art"><img alt="Artistic interpretation of a carved Indian temple corridor illuminated by golden morning light" height="1024" src="/assets/images/temple.webp" width="1536"/><figcaption>LIGHT, STONE &amp; LIVING TRADITION <span>ILLUSTRATIVE ARTWORK</span></figcaption></figure></section>
<div class="chapter-strip"><span>A PLACE FOR THE CURIOUS</span><p>Understand the ideas. Discover the traditions. Follow the stories.</p><span aria-hidden="true">✦</span></div>
<section class="section intro" id="understanding"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / THE FOUNDATIONS</p><h2>What is<br/><em>Sanatan Dharma?</em></h2></div><div class="intro-copy"><p class="lead">A diverse family of spiritual traditions, united by enduring questions about life, the self and the Divine.</p><p>Sanatana Dharma is a name used for Hinduism, often translated as “eternal dharma.” Its traditions encompass philosophy, devotion, ritual and ways of living. They share a rich inheritance while offering different interpretations of spiritual truth.</p><p>Begin with four ideas that appear throughout its teachings. Their meanings deepen across different texts and schools.</p><a class="source-link" href="/understanding.html">Read the full introduction</a></div></section>
<section aria-label="Four foundational ideas" class="concepts"><article><span class="concept-number">01</span><h3>Dharma</h3><p>Ethical living, responsibility and the principles that sustain life.</p></article><article><span class="concept-number">02</span><h3>Karma</h3><p>Action and its consequences, shaping the journey of life.</p></article><article><span class="concept-number">03</span><h3>Samsara</h3><p>The cycle of birth, death and rebirth in Hindu thought.</p></article><article><span class="concept-number">04</span><h3>Moksha</h3><p>Liberation from that cycle, understood differently across schools.</p></article></section>
<section class="section divine" id="divine"><div class="divine-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / THE DIVINE</p><h2>Many forms.<br/><em>Many ways of devotion.</em></h2><p>Divine traditions are an entrance into a much larger world of stories and thought. These are a few starting points, rather than a complete list.</p><a class="source-link divine-overview-link" href="/deities.html">Explore gods and goddesses</a></div><div class="divine-list"><article><span lang="sa">शिव</span><div><h3>Shiva</h3><p>Explore Shaiva traditions</p></div><a class="deity-home-link" href="/deities.html#shiva">Introduction</a></article><article><span lang="sa">विष्णु</span><div><h3>Vishnu</h3><p>Explore Vaishnava traditions</p></div><a class="deity-home-link" href="/deities.html#vishnu">Introduction</a></article><article><span lang="sa">देवी</span><div><h3>Devi</h3><p>Explore Shakta traditions</p></div><a class="deity-home-link" href="/deities.html#devi">Introduction</a></article></div><details class="question"><summary>How many gods and goddesses are there?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div><p>There is no single count that captures every Hindu tradition. Texts and communities describe the Divine in different ways, and many traditions connect multiple divine forms with one ultimate reality.</p><p>In the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad (3.9.1–9), a dialogue moves through several numbers—including thirty-three—and ultimately speaks of one. It invites reflection on the relationship between multiplicity and unity, rather than providing a universal catalogue.</p></div></details></section>
<section class="section scriptures" id="scriptures"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / SACRED LITERATURE</p><h2>A library of <em>perspectives.</em></h2></div><p>Hymns, philosophical dialogues, epic narratives and sacred stories. Each offers a different way into the tradition.</p></div><div class="text-grid"><article><span class="eyebrow">HYMNS &amp; SACRED KNOWLEDGE</span><h3>The Vedas</h3><p>Rig, Yajur, Sama and Atharva: four collections at the foundation of Hindu sacred literature.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">QUESTIONS OF THE SELF</span><h3>The Upanishads</h3><p>Philosophical explorations of the self, ultimate reality and liberation.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">LIFE, DUTY &amp; DEVOTION</span><h3>The Epics</h3><p>The Ramayana and Mahabharata; the Bhagavad Gita is part of the Mahabharata.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">SACRED NARRATIVES</span><h3>The Puranas</h3><p>Collections of narratives about deities, cosmic worlds, lineages and sacred places.</p></article></div><a class="scripture-overview-link" href="/scriptures.html">Explore Sacred Texts &amp; Scriptures →</a></section>
